            "text": "{\"primary_meaning\":\"A Semitic comparative idiom meaning to love less, to prioritize secondary to another, or to covenantally reject and not choose, rather than denoting literal emotional malice.\",\"primary_basis\":[\"Luke 14:26\",\"Romans 9:13\",\"Malachi 1:2-3\",\"Genesis 29:31-33\"],\"alternatives\":[{\"meaning\":\"Righteous indignation, absolute moral opposition, or divine wrath directed toward sin, idolatry, and injustice.\",\"basis\":[\"Proverbs 6:16-19\",\"Psalm 97:10\",\"Revelation 2:6\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"},{\"meaning\":\"Literal emotional animosity, enmity, or human malice, often portrayed negatively as a sin or positively in imprecatory contexts as loyalty to God.\",\"basis\":[\"Psalm 139:21-22\",\"Matthew 5:43\",\"1 John 2:9-11\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"}],\"has_settled_consensus\":true,\"confidence\":\"high\",\"notes\":\"Exegetes universally agree that in passages dealing with election (Jacob and Esau) or extreme discipleship (hating one's family), hate functions as a hyperbole or idiom for priority and preference. In other contexts, it can carry standard meanings of moral revulsion or human enmity.\"}",
